---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const title = "How to Calculate a Discount and Sale Price - Toolkit Lab";
const description = "How to work out a sale price from a percentage discount, how much you save, and how sales tax changes the final total.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/how-to-calculate-a-discount-and-sale-price.html">
  <aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
  <div class="wrap article">
    <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Shopping maths</p>
    <h1>How to Calculate a Discount and Sale Price</h1>
    <p class="lead">A 25% discount sounds simple, but it is easy to mix up the saving with the price you pay. A short calculation shows both, and tells you the total once tax is added.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>The two numbers you need</h2>
<p>The saving is the original price multiplied by the discount percentage, divided by 100. The sale price is the original price minus that saving. For an 80 item with 25% off, the saving is 20 and the sale price is 60.</p>
<h2>A quick check</h2>
<p>A discount of 50% always halves the price, and a discount of 10% takes one tenth off. If your answer does not match these shortcuts, recheck the arithmetic.</p>
<h2>Discounts and tax</h2>
<p>In most places, sales tax is added to the discounted price, not the original one. For example, a sale price of 60 with 8% tax costs 64.80 in total. Check the rules where you live, because some places handle it differently.</p>
<h2>Stacked discounts</h2>
<p>Two discounts applied one after the other do not add together. Take 20% off, then 10% off the new price. A 100 item becomes 80, then 72. That is a total discount of 28%, not 30%.</p>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>The <a href="/tools/discount-calculator.html">Discount Calculator</a> shows the sale price, the saving and the total with tax as you type.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>An 80 item with 25% off: the saving is 80 × 0.25 = 20, so the sale price is 60. With 8% sales tax added to the sale price, the total is 60 × 1.08 = 64.80.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>Do two discounts add together?</summary><p>No. A 20% discount followed by 10% off gives a total of 28%, not 30%. A 100 item becomes 80, then 72.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Is tax added before or after the discount?</summary><p>In many places tax is charged on the discounted price. Check the local rules, because some places handle it differently.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How do I work out the original price from a sale price?</summary><p>Divide the sale price by one minus the discount. A 60 sale price after 25% off was 60 ÷ 0.75 = 80.</p></details>
      </section>
